The attached image (Figure 3) illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site and is taken up by recruited cells. Panel A shows the kinetics of inflammatory cell recruitment, with Mac-2+ macrophages and Ly6C+ monocytes peaking around Day 2, while neutrophils decline rapidly after 6h. Panel B, through confocal images, demonstrates the intracellular uptake of Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 (red) by MHCII+ cells, Ly6C+ monocytes, and Mac-2+ macrophages at different time points post-injection, confirming its internalization by these immune cells. This supports Nano-11's role in local immune activation and antigen delivery.